ew York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Modern Library"},"dateIssued":"2011","edition":"[50th anniversary ed.]; 2011 Modern Library ed."},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xxxvi, 598 p. ; 19 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"The Death and Life of Great American Cities was described by The New York Times as \"perhaps the most influential single work in the history of town planning. ... [It] can also be seen in a much larger context. It is first of all a work of literature; the descriptions of street life as a kind of ballet and the bitingly satiric account of traditional planning theory can still be read for pleasure even by those who long ago absorbed and appropriated the book's arguments.\" Jane Jacobs, an editor and writer on architecture in New York City in the early sixties, argued that urban diversity and vitality were being destroyed by powerful architects and city planners. Rigorous, sane, and delightfully epigrammatic, Jane Jacobs's tour de force is a blueprint for the humanistic management of cities. It remains sensible, knowledgeable, readable, and indispensable. --- Book Description."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Partial contents","#text":"The peculiar nature of cities -- The conditions for city diversity -- Forces of decline and regeneration -- Different tactics."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Jane Jacobs ; with a new introduction by Jason Epstein and a foreword by the author."},"Originally published: New York : Ramdom House, 1961.","Includes index."],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-us---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"City planning","geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Urban renewal","geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Urban po